# Designing a Permission Layer — Menus, Buttons, and Routes

## 1. What is it?

A permission layer is the set of pieces that make an app's UI (and, critically, its route access) consistently respect a user's authorization — driven by data the backend returns after login, not hardcoded per screen. The question specifies the API returns "role and allowed menu items" after login; a robust design treats that as two related but distinct pieces of data:

- **Coarse-grained access (Big, broad, all-or-nothing checks) ** — which menu items / routes / screens a user can reach at all (`menuItems: string[]`).
- **Fine-grained access (Detailed, specific checks — down to individual actions, individual pieces of data, or individual fields/records)  ** — which individual actions/controls within a screen they're allowed to use (`actions: string[]`, e.g. `user.delete`, `report.export`).

The permission layer needs exactly three enforcement points wired to a single shared source of truth: a **route guard** (blocks navigation), a **structural directive** (hides/shows individual UI controls), and a **dynamically rendered menu** (doesn't show links to places the user can't go in the first place).

## 2. Why introduced? (why this is a real design problem, not just an if-check)

The naive approach — sprinkling `*ngIf="user.role === 'admin'"` through templates and one-off route guards per feature — breaks down fast in a real app:

- **Role checks scattered everywhere** means adding a new role or changing what a role can do requires hunting through every component that has a role check, instead of changing one place.
- **No single source of truth** — the sidebar might show a link that the route guard doesn't actually protect (or vice versa), because two different developers implemented them independently at different times.
- **Coupling UI visibility to business logic** (`role === 'admin' || role === 'manager'`) instead of to a specific capability makes it impossible to give one manager an extra permission without inventing a new role.
- **Security theater**: hiding a button is not the same as blocking the action — if the route guard doesn't independently enforce the same rule, a user can just type the URL.

Designing this as a layer — one service holding permission state, one guard, one directive, all driven by the same backend-issued data — solves all four problems at once and is the standard pattern in production Angular apps.

## 3. How does it work?

**Data contract from the login API:**

```typescript
interface PermissionsPayload {
  role: 'admin' | 'manager' | 'viewer';
  menuItems: string[]; // coarse-grained: which routes/sidebar links are reachable
  actions: string[];   // fine-grained: which individual buttons/controls render
}
```

**The four pieces, and how they connect:**

1. **`PermissionsService`** — the single source of truth. Holds the current payload (Angular signal), populated once by `AuthService.login()` right after the API call resolves. Exposes `hasMenu(key)`, `hasAction(key)`, and a `visibleMenu` computed signal (the full menu registry filtered down to what this user's `menuItems` includes).
2. **`permissionGuard`** (a single, reusable `CanActivateFn`) — routes opt in by declaring `data: { menuKey: 'users' }`. The guard reads that key off the route and checks it against `PermissionsService.hasMenu()`. No route needs a bespoke guard class; adding a new protected route is one line of route config.
3. **`HasPermissionDirective`** (`*appHasPermission="'user.delete'"`) — structurally identical to `*ngIf`, but checks `PermissionsService.hasAction()`. Gates individual buttons/controls independent of which route they're on.
4. **Dynamic menu rendering** — the sidebar iterates `permissions.visibleMenu()` instead of a hardcoded list with `*ngIf`s on each link. The menu registry (all *known* menu items, with their labels/paths) lives in code; which subset is *visible* comes entirely from the backend response.

**Why this design, specifically:**
- Splitting `menuItems` (routes/navigation) from `actions` (buttons/controls) mirrors how real backends usually model this and lets a route be reachable while still hiding specific actions inside it (e.g., a manager can view Reports but not export them) — one flat list of strings can't express that distinction cleanly.
- One data-driven guard function instead of N guard classes means the *policy* (what menuKey maps to what route) lives entirely in the route config, which is reviewable in one file.
- The directive re-evaluates via an Angular `effect()` watching the permission signal — not just on its own input binding changing — so if the app ever needs to refresh permissions without a full reload (e.g. after an admin changes someone's role mid-session), every gated button updates automatically.

**The critical security point:** the route guard and the directive are both **client-side, UX-layer enforcement**. They stop the Angular app from *rendering* a screen or control the user shouldn't see — they do **not**, and cannot, stop a user from calling the underlying API directly (via curl, devtools, a modified request). **The backend must independently re-validate every request against the same role/permission rules.** The client-side permission layer's job is to make the legitimate UI experience match what the backend will actually allow — never to be the only check.

## 4. Example   -->  https://stackblitz.com/edit/angular-e7f7r9hq-nqopp5hj?file=src%2Fapp%2Fapp.component.ts

```typescript
// permissions.service.ts — single source of truth
import { Injectable, computed, signal } from '@angular/core';
import { MENU_REGISTRY, MenuEntry, PermissionsPayload } from './models';

const STORAGE_KEY = 'demo_permissions';

@Injectable({ providedIn: 'root' })
export class PermissionsService {
  private state = signal<PermissionsPayload | null>(null);

  readonly isLoaded = computed(() => this.state() !== null);
  readonly role = computed(() => this.state()?.role ?? null);

  readonly visibleMenu = computed<MenuEntry[]>(() => {
    const current = this.state();
    if (!current) return [];
    return MENU_REGISTRY.filter((item) => current.menuItems.includes(item.key));
  });

  constructor() {
    const raw = sessionStorage.getItem(STORAGE_KEY);
    if (raw) {
      try { this.state.set(JSON.parse(raw)); }
      catch { sessionStorage.removeItem(STORAGE_KEY); }
    }
  }

  setPermissions(payload: PermissionsPayload) {
    this.state.set(payload);
    sessionStorage.setItem(STORAGE_KEY, JSON.stringify(payload));
  }

  clear() {
    this.state.set(null);
    sessionStorage.removeItem(STORAGE_KEY);
  }

  hasMenu(key: string): boolean {
    return this.state()?.menuItems.includes(key) ?? false;
  }

  hasAction(key: string): boolean {
    return this.state()?.actions.includes(key) ?? false;
  }
}
```

```typescript
// permission.guard.ts — one reusable, data-driven guard
import { inject } from '@angular/core';
import { CanActivateFn, Router } from '@angular/router';
import { PermissionsService } from './permissions.service';

export const permissionGuard: CanActivateFn = (route) => {
  const permissions = inject(PermissionsService);
  const router = inject(Router);

  if (!permissions.isLoaded()) {
    return router.parseUrl('/login');
  }

  const requiredMenuKey = route.data['menuKey'] as string | undefined;
  if (requiredMenuKey && !permissions.hasMenu(requiredMenuKey)) {
    return router.parseUrl('/unauthorized');
  }

  return true;
};
```

```typescript
// has-permission.directive.ts — fine-grained button/control gating
import { Directive, Input, TemplateRef, ViewContainerRef, effect, inject } from '@angular/core';
import { PermissionsService } from './permissions.service';

@Directive({ selector: '[appHasPermission]', standalone: true })
export class HasPermissionDirective {
  private permissions = inject(PermissionsService);
  private viewContainer = inject(ViewContainerRef);
  private templateRef = inject(TemplateRef<unknown>);
  private hasView = false;
  private action = '';

  @Input() set appHasPermission(action: string) {
    this.action = action;
    this.updateView();
  }

  constructor() {
    effect(() => {
      this.permissions.role(); // subscribe to permission-set changes
      this.updateView();
    });
  }

  private updateView() {
    const allowed = this.permissions.hasAction(this.action);
    if (allowed && !this.hasView) {
      this.viewContainer.createEmbeddedView(this.templateRef);
      this.hasView = true;
    } else if (!allowed && this.hasView) {
      this.viewContainer.clear();
      this.hasView = false;
    }
  }
}
```

```typescript
// app.routes.ts — the policy lives here, in one readable place
export const routes: Routes = [
  { path: 'login', loadComponent: () => import('./login.component').then((m) => m.LoginComponent) },
  { path: 'unauthorized', loadComponent: () => import('./unauthorized.component').then((m) => m.UnauthorizedComponent) },
  {
    path: '',
    component: ShellComponent,
    canActivate: [permissionGuard], // just "logged in at all"
    children: [
      { path: 'dashboard', data: { menuKey: 'dashboard' }, canActivate: [permissionGuard], loadComponent: () => import('./dashboard.component').then((m) => m.DashboardComponent) },
      { path: 'users', data: { menuKey: 'users' }, canActivate: [permissionGuard], loadComponent: () => import('./users.component').then((m) => m.UsersComponent) },
      { path: 'reports', data: { menuKey: 'reports' }, canActivate: [permissionGuard], loadComponent: () => import('./reports.component').then((m) => m.ReportsComponent) },
      { path: 'settings', data: { menuKey: 'settings' }, canActivate: [permissionGuard], loadComponent: () => import('./settings.component').then((m) => m.SettingsComponent) },
      { path: '', redirectTo: 'dashboard', pathMatch: 'full' },
